description:
Captures between a small game client and server that the course provides showed this structure (lesson 6.03): every message is a **2-byte big-endian length** (of everything after it), a **1-byte type**, a **4-byte big-endian sequence number**, then a payload:

| Type | Name | Payload |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | HELLO | 1-byte name length, then the name |
| 2 | MOVE | 2-byte big-endian signed dx, then dy |
| 3 | CHAT | 2-byte big-endian text length, then the text |
| 4 | BYE | nothing |

Write the decoder for a TCP stream, where messages can arrive split or joined:

```cpp
struct Message { std::uint8_t type; std::uint32_t sequence; std::vector<std::uint8_t> payload; };

class Decoder
{
public:
    void feed(const std::vector<std::uint8_t>& bytes);
    std::vector<Message> take();
    std::size_t pending() const;
};

std::string describe(const Message& m);
std::vector<std::uint32_t> missing(const std::vector<Message>& messages);
```

- **feed** adds received bytes; **take** returns every complete message received so far (in order) and removes them, leaving any incomplete tail; **pending** is how many bytes are waiting. A message whose length is below 5 cannot hold a type and a sequence: skip its whole frame.
- **describe** formats: `#7 HELLO Ada`, `#8 MOVE dx=3 dy=-1`, `#9 CHAT gg`, `#10 BYE`, and `#11 TYPE 9 (2 bytes)` for unknown types or payloads that do not match their type.
- **missing** lists the sequence numbers skipped between consecutive messages (7, 8, 11 is missing 9 and 10), in order.

